WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:25.230
As 
a marshal, Wild Bill Hickok took a hands-off

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.230
approach.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.980
He spent most of his day gambling in the saloon
and his deputies knew where to find him if

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.120
they needed him.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.309
And even though he policed some of the most
lawless towns out in the west, this technique

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:37.430
worked more often than not.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.760
Even back then, his name and reputation were
enough to make other gunslingers think twice

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.370
about causing trouble in the town where Wild
Bill was the law.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:51.339
A lot of the legend and mystique surrounding
Hickok was created by magazines (Harper’s

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:55.390
Monthly, in particular), but there was a time
when Hickok might genuinely have been the

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.230
most dangerous gunman in the Old West.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:03.309
His list of victims included a lot of violent
men (and one bear, but we’ll get to that

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:04.559
in a minute).

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:10.360
The tales about him may have been exaggerated
to an insane degree, and yet the real stories

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:19.340
about Wild Bill Hickok were still full of
mayhem, murder, and adventure.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.270
Early Years

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:28.960
He was born James Butler Hickok on May 27,
1837, in a little town called Homer, Illinois,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.220
which has since been renamed to Troy Grove.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:36.920
He came from a big family and had four brothers
and two sisters and his parents were William

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.689
Alonzo Hickok and Polly Butler.

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:43.659
If young James had a sense of justice and
bravery, they were likely instilled in him

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:48.469
by his father who was a staunch abolitionist
and even worked with the Underground Railroad

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:50.400
to smuggle slaves out of the South.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.719
In later stories, Wild Bill claimed that sometimes
he would accompany his father on smuggle runs

00:01:54.719 --> 00:01:57.869
and had his fair share of close calls with
the law.

00:01:57.869 --> 00:02:02.840
On May 5, 1852, William Hickok died, although
it is unclear if it was related to his abolitionist

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.009
activities or not.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:08.110
James and his three older brothers now had
to take care of the farm and the family.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.130
From his early teens, Hickok proved himself
quite proficient with guns.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:15.290
As a teenager, he would often go hunting to
provide food for the family.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:20.280
James found his first job as a drover on the
Illinois and Michigan Canal, but soon enough

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.360
he headed westward to look for better opportunities.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.470
This will be a recurring theme in Hickok’s
life as the man never seemed able to settle

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.950
down in one place for long.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.130
According to some versions of the story, Hickok
left because he had to, not because he wanted

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:37.910
to, after getting into a fight with another
drover named Charles Hudson.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:42.560
Hudson was a bully who often targeted the
younger James for his “practical jokes.”

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.970
One day, Hickok had had enough and the two
began wrestling and ended up in the canal.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:51.010
When James emerged, his opponent was still
in the water and did not appear to be breathing.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.930
Fearing that he had killed him, Hickok ran
away from the scene and fled the state, although

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.000
he later discovered that Hudson had survived
their encounter.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.270
It didn’t matter, anyway, as he soon found
something more to his liking.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:06.540
Hickok ended up in Kansas during the brief
seven-year period between 1854 and 1861 when

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.960
it had been incorporated as a territory but
had not become a state yet.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.060
This period is generally known as Bleeding
Kansas because it was marked by violent confrontations

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:21.060
between pro-slavery and anti-slavery groups
since that was the hot-button issue for the

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.360
soon-to-be state.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.680
Unsurprisingly, Hickok was on the anti-slavery
side.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.050
He joined up with a militia led by future
Union General Jim Lane which was called the

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.370
Free State Army, but became much better known
as the “Jayhawkers.”

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:38.020
Allegedly, it was during this time that James
saved the life of a boy, no older than 11

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.610
or 12 years old, by the name of William Cody.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:43.160
He would grow up to be Buffalo Bill.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.290
Hickok’s reputation as a gunfighter slowly
began to build.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:50.650
He served as constable for Monticello Township
for a while, before accepting a position with

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:55.550
the Russell, Majors, and Waddell Freight Company,
best known as the parent company for the Pony

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:56.660
Express mail service.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.010
It was at this time that the legend of Wild
Bill Hickok almost ended before it got started

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.150
when the gunfighter was mauled by a bear.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.510
Some versions of the story (including Hickok’s
own account) said that the stagecoach ran

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.210
into a cinnamon bear blocking the road.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:15.640
Hickok got out and shot it, but failed to
kill the animal and a fight ensued.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:19.580
Another, less courageous telling asserts that
Hickok’s party was sleeping in the wild

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:23.210
and the bear simply stumbled onto their camp
and attacked Wild Bill.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.990
Both stories ended the same way - Hickok managed
to kill the bear with his knife, but not before

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.900
it did some serious damage.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:35.259
Bill had to recuperate for months and, afterwards,
he was sent to Rock Creek Station.

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:40.710
The McCanles Massacre

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:45.090
We arrive at, perhaps, the most controversial
episode from Wild Bill’s life, one that

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:50.889
could potentially change the perception of
Hickok from gunfighter to cold-blooded murderer.

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:55.509
Rock Creek Station, as its name implies, was
a station for the Pony Express and regular

00:04:55.509 --> 00:04:59.169
stagecoaches in the southeastern part of the
Nebraska Territory.

00:04:59.169 --> 00:05:03.439
It was built on land that used to be owned
by a man named David McCanles who sold his

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:05.780
property to the freight company in installments.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:09.800
If we go by the popular version of the story,
McCanles was also an outlaw who led his own

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.160
gang responsible for robberies, cattle rustling,
and even murder.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.560
At one point, the company was struggling financially
and failed to pay an installment on time.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:22.870
On July 12, 1861, McCanles and two henchmen,
James Woods and James Gordon, went to the

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.789
station to get the money he was owed.

00:05:24.789 --> 00:05:28.650
There, he found the station manager, Horace
Wellman, and his two employees, a man called

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.830
Brink and Hickok who worked there as a stock
tender.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.699
Also present was McCanles’ 12-year-old son,
Monroe.

00:05:34.699 --> 00:05:39.080
It is impossible to tell with any certainty
what the exact circumstances were.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.919
Some say that McCanles became verbally abusive
towards the station employees, or that he

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:46.550
forced his way inside to look for money, or
that he pulled out a gun.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:51.189
Others say that McCanles and his men were
unarmed and that Wellman came out with a shotgun,

00:05:51.189 --> 00:05:53.810
or that Hickok opened fire without provocation.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.039
Because Wild Bill became a western icon, the
version that made him look like a hero was

00:05:58.039 --> 00:05:59.840
the one that circulated the most.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.010
Either way, the meeting turned into a confrontation
which turned into a gunfight.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:07.740
Soon enough, McCanles had been shot dead and
both his men were fatally injured.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.229
Nobody on the other side sustained serious
injuries.

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:13.460
We don’t know for sure who shot who.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:18.039
Hickok usually gets the credit for killing
McCanles, although some versions say Wellman.

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:22.150
Both men plus Brink were charged with three
murders, but were acquitted on the basis of

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:24.810
self-defense and defense of company property.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:29.870
Interestingly, Monroe McCanles always sustained
that his father and his two men were not armed

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:33.979
and were gunned down in cold blood, but he
was not allowed to testify at the trial due

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:34.979
to his age.

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:37.249
Here’s an interesting bit of trivia for
you.

00:06:37.249 --> 00:06:42.500
David McCanles might have been dead, but his
legacy continued on to great success.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:47.849
His descendants changed their name to McCandless
and included two decorated war heroes, as

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:53.249
well as Captain Bruce McCandless II, the astronaut
who performed the first untethered spaceflight

00:06:53.249 --> 00:06:54.249
in history.

00:06:54.249 --> 00:06:59.059
After the episode at Rock Creek Station concluded,
Hickok enlisted in the Union Army in the Civil

00:06:59.059 --> 00:07:00.059
War.

00:07:00.059 --> 00:07:02.750
He first served as a teamster and wagon master.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:06.559
Afterwards, Bill joined up with an old acquaintance
of his, General Jim Lane, former leader of

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:10.110
the Jayhawkers, who was now in command of
the Kansas Brigade.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.830
At this point, Hickok also reunited with an
old friend of his, Buffalo Bill Cody who was

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:16.530
working as a scout.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.360
Hickok wore many hats during the war, going
where he was needed.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:24.210
He served as scout, spy, and sharpshooter,
among others, and distinguished himself at

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.669
the Battle of Pea Ridge, Arkansas.

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:31.319
This might be the time when Hickok got his
“Wild Bill” moniker.

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:35.770
As, arguably, the most famous nickname of
the Old West, many historians have wondered

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:39.830
when and why James Butler Hickok became known
as “Wild Bill.”

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.160
Multiple sources agree that the gunfighter
obtained the moniker during the war for his

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.050
daring, but reckless ways, but the exact origins
are up for debate.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:54.280
Also uncertain is why he would be called Bill
in the first place since his name was James.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:58.009
According to documents of that era, for a
time, he went by the name William Haycock

00:07:58.009 --> 00:08:00.210
so that could be the explanation.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:04.699
Another possible alternative was that the
“Bill” wasn’t originally short for William,

00:08:04.699 --> 00:08:08.159
but rather “bill” as in the beak of a
bird.

00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:12.830
Before he was known as Wild Bill, he was sometimes
called Duck Bill because he had a long, sharp

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:15.240
nose and a protruding upper lip.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.259
We know this for certain because he is referred
to as “Duck Bill” in the court documents

00:08:19.259 --> 00:08:21.080
for the McCanles trial.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.749
Hickok absolutely hated this nickname.

00:08:23.749 --> 00:08:28.029
He grew out his moustache to cover his lip
and it is feasible that he started going by

00:08:28.029 --> 00:08:58.520
William just so he’d have another reason
to be

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:41.660
called Bill.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:10:01.090
The Gunfight that Defined the Wild West

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:05.461
The event that turned James Butler Hickok
into Wild Bill Hickok, legend of the Wild

00:10:05.461 --> 00:10:09.690
West, occurred soon after the man arrived
in Springfield, Missouri, following the Civil

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:10.690
War.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.840
If you have ever seen a quickdraw duel between
two men in a western movie, it is because

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:19.090
of what happened here because they certainly
were not common in the real West.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:24.160
Hickok quickly developed an antagonistic relationship
with another fellow gambler named Davis Tutt,

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.200
a former confederate soldier.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.560
According to some acquaintances, the two men
may have been friends at first, despite the

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.160
fact that they served on opposite sides of
the Civil War.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.420
Whatever dispute came between them we don’t
know (some say it may have been a woman),

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:40.080
but it caused plenty of bad blood between
the two gamblers.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:44.330
Some sources mention that Hickok refused to
play cards at the same table as Tutt so the

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:49.750
latter started loaning money to other gamblers
and giving them tips on how to beat Wild Bill.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:53.950
Things weren’t going in Tutt’s favor on
the afternoon of July 20, 1865.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:57.530
In fact, Hickok had won around $200 of his
money.

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:01.400
To try and put him off his game, Tutt began
taunting the gambler, reminding Hickok that

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.900
he owed him $40 from a horse trade.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:08.430
According to witnesses, Wild Bill agreed and
settled his debt right then and there, but

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.450
this wasn’t enough for Tutt.

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:14.600
He then said that Bill owed him another $35
from a different transaction.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:19.510
Hickok disputed this claim, mentioning that,
according to his notes, he only owed $25 which

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:20.640
he offered to pay.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:24.830
As you might imagine, because Tutt regularly
gave out loans to gamblers in need, he was

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:25.880
a pretty popular guy.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:30.190
In fact, a lot of his friends were there that
night and they all had guns.

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:33.360
Sensing that he had the upper hand, Tutt decided
to get cocky.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:38.190
He walked up to Hickok’s card table and
snatched his Waltham gold pocket watch in

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:39.190
front of everybody.

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:44.080
He then loudly proclaimed that he will keep
it as collateral until he got the other $10.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:48.280
Obviously, Wild Bill was furious, but there
was little he could do as he was completely

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:49.280
outgunned.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.810
But the situation had other serious consequences
for him than just a public humiliation.

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:58.380
At that point, his primary occupation was
gambling and he could not afford to develop

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:03.160
a reputation as someone who was unable or
unwilling to settle his debts because nobody

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:05.060
would want to play against him anymore.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:10.420
So Hickok allowed Tutt to walk away with his
watch, but warned him not to wear it in public.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:14.330
Looking back at it, this is where Tutt probably
should have called it a day and quit while

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:15.330
he was ahead.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:19.740
It is unclear if he said this on the spot
or conveyed the message to Bill later through

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:24.450
his associates, but word soon spread that
Tutt was planning to stroll through the town

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:28.120
square the next day, proudly showing off his
new, shiny watch.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.990
Hickok replied that he would only do this
if “dead men can walk.”

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:35.160
At this point, Tutt was backed into a corner.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:38.390
We can only speculate on his ideal outcome
of the matter.

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:43.880
Did he actually want to goad Wild Bill into
a gunfight or did he simply want to humiliate

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:45.570
him as much as possible?

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.540
Whatever his intentions were, he pushed too
far.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:52.420
Everyone in Springfield knew what he did and
what he bragged that he was going to do so,

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:55.440
if he backed off, he would have been seen
as a coward.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.840
The next morning, the two men met and, according
to witness testimony, neither one seemed eager

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:00.840
for a fight.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.810
They argued for a while and, eventually, went
for a drink together.

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:07.580
For a bit, it looked like the matter might
be settled amicably, but their little get-together

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:09.880
ended with Tutt storming off angrily.

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:13.810
What they said we will never know, but that
afternoon, Tutt could be seen in front of

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:16.130
the Springfield courthouse with Hickok’s
watch.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:20.090
Word eventually reached Wild Bill who knew
that the matter had to end one way or another.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:25.050
At around 6 p.m., he walked out in the town
square and headed towards the courthouse and

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:26.340
called out Tutt.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:30.210
Everybody else rushed off to the sides and
only the two gunslingers were left standing

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:31.690
in the middle of the street.

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.070
Just like in the movies.

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:37.470
Hickok and Tutt exchanged a few words, hesitated
for a bit, but then pulled out their guns

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.810
and fired at almost the exact same time.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:41.490
We cannot say who shot first.

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:45.820
Some witnesses claimed Tutt shot as fast as
possible while Bill took the time to steady

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:46.820
his aim.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:50.900
If this is true, then it paid off for Hickok
because Tutt missed, while he hit him between

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:53.870
the ribs, causing Tutt to collapse and die.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:57.570
The shot itself was incredibly impressive
which is partially why it caused a sensation.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:14:02.570
The two men were allegedly standing 75 yards
apart, at a time when your average revolver

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:04.620
began losing accuracy after 25 yards.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:07.670
The gunfight became the stuff of legends.

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:12.510
It turned Wild Bill Hickok into an icon of
the Old West, but this did not happen overnight.

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:17.210
In fact, it wasn’t until early 1867 that
an article in Harper’s New Monthly Magazine

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:20.540
about the shootout propelled Hickok to national
fame.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:25.240
The article was written by George Ward Nichols
who, honestly, deserves about as much credit

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:29.450
for creating the legend of Wild Bill as Hickok
himself.

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:33.720
The article was incredibly sensationalistic
and was far more concerned with telling a

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.960
compelling story than telling the truth.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:40.450
It contained several falsehoods including,
not in the least, naming the gunfighter Hitchcock

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:43.550
instead of Hickok, but it got the job done.

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:48.190
Nichols was also the man who sensationalized
the previous shootout with David McCanles

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:52.600
to the point where Wild Bill fought off a
bloodthirsty gang of a dozen men single-handedly,

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:56.390
armed only with a rifle and a Bowie knife.

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:59.850
One part of the duel that you won’t often
see in the movies is the aftermath.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:03.950
Hickok had just gunned down a man in front
of the entire town so he was arrested and

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:05.240
charged with murder.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.900
The charge was later downgraded to manslaughter
and Bill managed to post bail.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:13.150
His trial took place at the start of August
and, after hearing from 22 eyewitnesses, the

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:14.250
jury acquitted him.

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:17.810
This verdict was not popular with everyone
and the jury had been accused by multiple

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:23.220
publications of conceding to the unwritten
frontier law of a “fair fight” instead

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:28.460
of the government laws which would have clearly
found Hickok guilty.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:33.170
Marshal Hickok

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:37.050
Now that the business with Tutt was dealt
with, Hickok thought about going into law

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:38.050
enforcement.

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:42.360
He first ran for city marshal in the same
city of Springfield, but lost the election.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:47.450
In 1867, he took a brief hiatus from his policing
ambitions and joined the Indian Wars, serving

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:48.470
as a scout again.

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:52.400
Here, he worked under General George Custer
who, a decade later, would gain infamy for

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:57.300
his defeat at the Battle of Little Bighorn,
also known as “Custer’s Last Stand.”

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.740
Hickok made a deep impression on the general
who went on to describe him as “one of the

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:05.280
most perfect types of physical manhood I ever
saw...His skill in the use of the rifle and

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:11.260
pistol was unerring...His influence among
the frontiersmen was unbounded; his word was

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:12.260
law.”

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:16.780
General Custer reported that Wild Bill had
two large ivory-handled revolvers which he

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:21.860
always carried with him and that, at the time,
Hickok was responsible for at least six killings

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:26.470
in shootouts and many other injuries, yet
he had always escaped untouched.

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:30.550
In 1869, Wild Bill became the city marshal
of Hays, Kansas.

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:34.950
At the same time, he was also serving as sheriff
of Ellis County after having been elected

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:38.160
to finish the remaining term of the previous
sheriff who quit.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:42.190
It didn’t take long before Hickok began
setting down the law in his own way.

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:44.600
He killed two men in his first month as sheriff.

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:49.810
First was a troublemaker named Bill Mulvey
or Melvin who got drunk and began shooting

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:55.170
out random windows in town and the other was
a man who got into a bar fight named Samuel

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:56.170
Strawhun.

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:59.070
Both men drew on Hickok...both men lost.

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:03.590
Another violent encounter happened in 1870
when two drunk cavalry troopers attacked the

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:05.490
marshal, prompting him to return fire.

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:09.860
One of the men had his knee shattered while
the other died of his injuries the next day.

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:13.959
It seemed that the people of Ellis County
weren’t very happy with Hickok’s tactics

00:17:13.959 --> 00:17:15.970
and he lost the next election.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:20.730
The gunfighter moved on and became marshal
in Abilene, Kansas, a city with a much wilder

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:21.730
reputation.

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:26.669
In fact, the previous marshal, a notable gunman
in his own right named Tom “Bear River”

00:17:26.669 --> 00:17:31.539
Smith, had not only been killed while serving
a warrant, but also decapitated.

00:17:31.539 --> 00:17:34.860
This was a city more suited to Hickok’s
trigger-happy disposition.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:39.830
In fact, soon after becoming marshal, he was
involved in a saloon shootout where two men

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:40.830
were killed.

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:44.679
Afterwards, the local newspaper published
an article defending the marshal, making it

00:17:44.679 --> 00:17:47.950
clear that that was the new standard procedure
from then on.

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:52.659
It read: “There is no use in trying to override
Wild Bill, the Marshal.

00:17:52.659 --> 00:17:56.809
His arrangements for policing the city are
complete, and attempts to kill police officers

00:17:56.809 --> 00:18:02.299
or in any way create disturbance, must result
in loss of life on the part of violators of

00:18:02.299 --> 00:18:03.410
the law."

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:06.919
During this time, Wild Bill had an encounter
with John Wesley Hardin.

00:18:06.919 --> 00:18:08.280
You may not have heard of Hardin.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:13.409
He wasn’t as famous as some other contemporary
outlaws like Jesse James or Billy the Kid,

00:18:13.409 --> 00:18:14.929
but he had a higher kill count.

00:18:14.929 --> 00:18:19.480
He proclaimed that he gunned down over 40
men, although other sources credited him with

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.740
“only” 27 kills.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:25.279
He wasn’t as prolific when he visited Abilene,
although he was still an outlaw.

00:18:25.279 --> 00:18:30.379
We’re not sure exactly how the event unfolded
as we don’t have Hickok’s side of the

00:18:30.379 --> 00:18:34.140
story and Hardin gave differing accounts over
the years.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:38.169
What we do know happened was that Hardin killed
the man sleeping in the hotel room next to

00:18:38.169 --> 00:18:39.990
his for snoring too loud.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:44.580
When Wild Bill came to confront him, one of
two things probably took place (at least,

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:45.580
according to Hardin).

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:50.250
He either got the drop on the marshal, disarmed
him and left town, or the likelier version

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:54.700
- he jumped out the window dressed only in
his undershirt, hid in a haystack, stole a

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.149
horse and rode away.

00:18:56.149 --> 00:19:01.259
We never got a gunfight between Wild Bill
and Hardin, but we did get one between Hickok

00:19:01.259 --> 00:19:04.460
and Phil Coe, the owner of the Bull’s Head
Saloon in town.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:09.040
We’re not sure on the source of the animosity
between them, but it escalated to violence

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:11.690
and Coe drew on Hickok during a street brawl.

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:16.750
Hickok drew back, shot and killed Coe, but
then he made the worst mistake of his life.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:20.730
Wild Bill noticed some sudden movement and,
out of pure instinct, he shot and killed the

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:22.039
man heading towards him.

00:19:22.039 --> 00:19:33.600
However, that man was Mike Williams, one of
his friends and deputies.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:34.600
The Dead Man’s Hand

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:38.049
Unsurprisingly, Hickok was dismissed as marshal
for his lethal blunder and, to our knowledge,

00:19:38.049 --> 00:19:39.799
he never killed a man again.

00:19:39.799 --> 00:19:43.610
Besides his guilt for Williams’ death, he
was dealing with another big problem - he

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:45.059
was losing his vision.

00:19:45.059 --> 00:19:49.999
Some say he had glaucoma, others ophthalmia,
but he would have probably gone blind if he

00:19:49.999 --> 00:19:51.350
would have lived longer.

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.570
His gunfighting days were over.

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:57.590
Wild Bill’s last few years were unbefitting
of a man once regarded as one of the greatest

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:59.080
legends of the Old West.

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:01.330
He was arrested for vagrancy several times.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:05.110
He married an older widow whom he left after
just a few months.

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:09.340
For a while, he joined Buffalo Bill’s Wild
West Show, but found that he hated performing

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:13.080
on stage and only lasted a couple of months
before being fired.

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:18.730
In 1876, Wild Bill joined a man named Charlie
Utter and went prospecting for gold in Deadwood,

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:19.789
South Dakota.

00:20:19.789 --> 00:20:24.249
On August 2, he was shot in the back of the
head while playing poker by a drunken gambler

00:20:24.249 --> 00:20:25.669
named Jack McCall.

00:20:25.669 --> 00:20:30.360
According to legend, he was holding the black
pairs of aces and eights which has since become

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:49.120
known as 
the “dead man’s hand.”

